content:
{
  "adjustments": [
    {
      "target": "u1",
      "focus": ["D", "S", "C"],
      "change": "Strengthen and widen the anti-repetition/coverage rule: keep it active when the needed object has not been acquired or a placement leaves the task unmet, but stop searching once the exact target is held and proceed with clean/transform/place. Use the last two pre-action pairs to detect recent no-progress visits/toggles and choose a different location from the current admissible actions. Cover both closed containers and broad surfaces by name, including cabinets, drawers, shelves, basins, countertops, diningtables, beds, sidetables, desks, and toilets, so a diningtable-like surface is not skipped merely because it is not a closed container. Preserve the multi-instance return to a known location that still contains a needed visible instance.",
      "reason": "Current `P7` and `P8` both end at step_limit after cycling over a small set while the exact target is still missing (`P7:50`, `P8:50`). `P7` takes a knife rather than a butterknife at `P7:5`, cleans and moves it at `P7:7`/`P7:9`, then repeats drawers and countertops without opening the many closed cabinets. `P8` repeatedly returns to cabinet1/cabinet2/cabinet3, countertop1, and sinkbasin1 (`P8:34`, `P8:50`) while other named surfaces are not covered. This supports retaining the anti-loop/coverage direction, narrowing its active phase to before acquisition or after an unmet placement, and widening coverage beyond closed containers. `M2` reports a similar search edit where a candidate failed after never visiting diningtable1 (`S1_R02/Q8:50`) and other candidates still rechecked known-empty locations, so a pure unvisited-type rule was insufficient; the revision therefore uses the only available recent-history window, current admissible action names, and explicit broad-surface coverage. The boundary to proceed once the exact target is held preserves direct clean/cool/place patterns such as `P6:5`, `P9:5`, and `P10:12`, and the final sentence preserves the Parent multi-instance return rule. Unverified choice: last-two-pair recency cannot guarantee global coverage under the Actor's limited history.",
      "to": {
        "patch": "--- a/SKILL.md\n+++ b/SKILL.md\n@@\n-- Keep the anti-repetition and coverage search discipline active whenever the task's needed object is still not found or not yet placed, including single-object tasks such as \"put a clean <object> in <receptacle>\", not only multi-instance tasks.\n-- If recent observations show you just visited, opened, or toggled a candidate location and it did not reveal the needed object, choose a different candidate location instead of returning to it.\n-- Cover candidate container and surface types you have not yet checked in the current scene, including opening closed cabinets and drawers and checking shelves and other receptacles, rather than cycling through the same small subset of locations.\n-- If you detect you have returned to an already-checked location without new progress, break the loop by going to a still-unvisited candidate location.\n+- Keep the anti-repetition and coverage search discipline active whenever the task's needed object has not been acquired, or after a placement leaves the task unmet, including single-object tasks such as \"put a clean <object> in <receptacle>\", not only multi-instance tasks. Once the exact target is held, proceed with the required clean, transform, or placement rather than continuing to search; after placing one instance in a multi-instance task, resume the search if more are required.\n+- If the last two pre-action observation/action pairs show you just visited, opened, or toggled a candidate location and it did not reveal the needed object, choose a different candidate location from the current admissible actions instead of returning to it.\n+- Cover candidate containers and surfaces by name from the current admissible actions rather than cycling through the same small subset: include closed cabinets and drawers, shelves, basins, countertops, diningtables, beds, sidetables, desks, toilets, and other named receptacles. Do not omit a broad surface such as a diningtable merely because it is not a closed container, and do not ignore closed cabinets or drawers.\n+- If the last two pre-action pairs show a return to an already checked location or another no-progress toggle, break the loop by preferring a named candidate location whose type is not among the locations in those recent pairs; alternate between closed containers and broad surfaces until the exact target is found or the task is complete. This anti-repetition rule does not override returning to a known location that still contains a needed visible instance for a multi-instance task.\n"
      }
    },
    {
      "target": "u2",
      "focus": ["D", "S", "C"],
      "change": "Strengthen exact-type selection from a warning not to treat similar objects as the target into a pre-taking full-name check: compare the object name in the observation or inventory to the task noun before taking it, and leave similar-but-different objects such as knife instead of butterknife, pan instead of pot, or cup instead of mug. Before cleaning, transforming, or placing, confirm the held object is the exact type; if not, do not use it as the target and resume searching. If the task remains unmet after placing an object in the named target receptacle, treat that as evidence of a wrong-type object or a still-needed additional exact instance, and resume exact-target search with the same broad coverage discipline rather than repeating the placement or cycling locations.",
      "reason": "Current `P7` directly supports this: the task requires a butterknife, but the Actor takes knife1 at `P7:5`, cleans it at `P7:7`, moves it to drawer1 with no reward at `P7:9`, and then never recovers before step_limit (`P7:50`). This shows the exact-type check must apply before taking, not only before cleaning/placing, and that an unmet placement should trigger exact-target resumption. `M3` reports historical candidate failures where similar-object substitution persisted despite an exact-type clause, including pan-for-pot and cup-for-mug attempts (`S1_R03/Q7:49`, `S1_R03/Q10:25`), so the revision adds full-name comparison and explicit leave/continue guidance. Coordination with revised u1 routes post-placement recovery into broad coverage rather than repeated cycles. The examples and pre-taking comparison are unverified extensions, but they are low-cost because object names are visible, and they address directly observed wrong-type failures. Preserved behavior includes direct clean/cool-and-place sequences such as `P6:5`, `P9:5`, and `P10:12`, where the exact type was already correctly selected.",
      "to": {
        "patch": "--- a/SKILL.md\n+++ b/SKILL.md\n@@\n-- Take and use the exact object type named in the task; if the visible objects are similar but of a different type (for example a knife where a butterknife is required), do not treat the wrong type as the target.\n-- Before cleaning, transforming, or placing the required object, confirm the object you are holding matches the exact type named in the task.\n-- If the task remains unmet after you place an object, do not repeat the same placement or cycle the same locations; resume the search for the exact named target object, including inside closed cabinets and drawers.\n+- Take and use only the exact object type named in the task; compare the object's full name in the observation or inventory to the task noun before taking it. If the visible objects are similar but of a different type (for example a knife where a butterknife is required, a pan where a pot is required, or a cup where a mug is required), leave them and continue searching; do not treat the wrong type as the target.\n+- Before cleaning, transforming, or placing the required object, confirm the object you are holding matches the exact type named in the task. If it does not, do not clean, transform, or place it as the target; resume the search for the exact type.\n+- If the task remains unmet after you place an object in the named target receptacle, treat that as a signal that the placed object was not the required exact type or that another exact instance is still needed. Do not repeat the same placement or cycle the same locations; resume the search for the exact named target object with the same broad coverage discipline, including inside closed cabinets and drawers and on unvisited broad surfaces.\n"
      }
    }
  ],
  "checks": [
    {
      "id": "c1",
      "update_ids": ["u1"],
      "question": "When the needed object is not held or the task remains unmet after a placement, does the Actor avoid returning to locations shown in the last two pre-action pairs to have been checked without progress, and instead choose a different named candidate location, covering both closed containers and broad surfaces such as diningtables, countertops, shelves, basins, and toilets?"
    },
    {
      "id": "c2",
      "update_ids": ["u2"],
      "question": "Does the Actor take and use only the exact task-named object type rather than a similar substitute, confirm the exact type before cleaning/transforming/placing, and after an unmet placement resume exact-target search with the coverage discipline rather than repeating the same placement or cycling the same locations?"
    },
    {
      "id": "c3",
      "update_ids": ["u1", "u2"],
      "question": "When the exact target is already held or is found at the first checked location, do the revised rules preserve the direct acquire-then-activate, acquire-then-place, and acquire-clean/cool-then-place sequences, including direct clean at a sinkbasin and direct cool at a fridge before placement?"
    }
  ]
}
